<script>
const content = {
  title: {
    en: "Why Is the Sky Blue?",
    zh: "为什么天空是蓝色的？"
  },
  columns: [
    {
      lang: "en",
      label: "English",
      tagline: "A bilingual explanation",
      entries: [
        {
          icon: "🧒",
          heading: "For a 3-Year-Old Toddler",
          text: "The sky is blue because of tiny specks in the air! Sunshine is white, like a sparkly ball of many colors. When it hits the air, the blue colors bounce around and everywhere — so even when you look away from the sun, the sky all over is blue. It's like the air is a giant blue balloon!"
        },
        {
          icon: "🎓",
          heading: "For a High School Teenager",
          text: "Sunlight contains all the colors of the rainbow. As it enters Earth's atmosphere, it bumps into tiny molecules of air (mostly nitrogen and oxygen). These molecules scatter light, and the scattering is much stronger for shorter wavelengths — like blue and violet light. That's why blue light gets bounced in every direction, lighting up the sky blue all over. Violet light scatters even more, but our eyes are less sensitive to it and the sun emits slightly less violet, so the sky looks blue. (At sunrise and sunset, sunlight passes through more air, red light scatters less, and the sky turns orange and red.)"
        },
        {
          icon: "🔬",
          heading: "For a Physics PhD",
          text: "The blue color of the sky results from Rayleigh scattering of sunlight by atmospheric molecules much smaller than the wavelength of light. The scattering intensity follows an inverse-fourth-power wavelength dependence: I ∝ 1/λ⁴. Since violet (~400 nm) and blue (~450 nm) wavelengths scatter far more strongly than red/orange (~650–700 nm), the scattered light reaching an observer is concentrated toward the blue end of the visible spectrum. Although violet scatters more, the solar spectrum peaks in green/blue and the eye's photopic sensitivity (and the chromatic response of the cones) weights the mixture toward blue, so the perceived sky color is blue rather than violet. Scattering is also approximately dipole-like, giving a slightly brighter sky toward the horizon (greater path length through the air) and polarization of scattered light at 90° from the sun. Complex atmospheric composition, aerosols (which add whitish/hazy scattering), and higher-order effects play secondary roles."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      lang: "zh",
      label: "中文",
      tagline: "双语解释",
      entries: [
        {
          icon: "🧒",
          heading: "给 3 岁的小宝宝",
          text: "天空是蓝色的，是因为空气中有很多小小的颗粒！阳光是白色的，像一个闪亮的、由许多颜色组成的小球。当它照到空气中时，蓝色的光就会到处都弹来弹去——所以即使你看向太阳以外的地方，天空也是蓝的。好像空气是一个巨大的蓝色气球！"
        },
        {
          icon: "🎓",
          heading: "给高中生",
          text: "阳光包含了彩虹的所有颜色。当它进入地球的大气层时，会与空气中微小的分子（主要是氮气和氧气）碰撞。这些分子会散射光，而较短波长的光（比如蓝光和紫光）散射得强得多。这就是为什么蓝光会朝各个方向被散射，让整个天空都显得蓝色。紫光散射得更强，但我们的眼睛对它不太敏感，而且太阳发出的紫光也略少一些，所以天空看起来是蓝色的，而不是紫色的。（在日出和日落时，阳光要穿过更多的空气，红光散射得较少，所以天空会变成橙色和红色。）"
        },
        {
          icon: "🔬",
          heading: "给物理博士",
          text: "天空呈蓝色是由于太阳光经过比光波长小得多的大气分子所发生的瑞利散射。散射强度遵循与波长的四次方成反比的规律：I ∝ 1/λ⁴。由于紫光（约 400 nm）和蓝光（约 450 nm）散射得远比红光/橙光（约 650–700 nm）强烈，到达观察者眼中的散射光主要集中在可见光谱的蓝端。虽然紫光散射更强，但太阳光谱主要峰值在绿/蓝区域，且人眼的明视觉（以及锥状细胞的色觉响应）使混合光偏向蓝色，所以感知到的天空颜色是蓝色而不是紫色。散射还近似为偶极子型，这使得向地平线方向的天空略为明亮（空气路径更长），并且散射光在与太阳成 90° 方向时具有偏振特性。大气成分的复杂性、气溶胶（带来发白/雾状的散射）以及高阶效应则只起次要作用。"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
};

const main = document.getElementById('content');

content.columns.forEach(col => {
